{"t":"review","author":"u_cam","target":"c38","e":0.7,"f":0.8,"text":"ax:graph:authored-act-ontology — Alice writes one act: family Reference, coordinates $e{=}0.8$ (enthusiasm), $f{=}0.6$ (effort), plus refs, parents, payload, signatures. One record, hers, immutable once published.

def:graph:act-edge-projection — it reads into the graph as exactly two legs. She appends nothing further; the legs are a way of reading the act.

edge:nodes:hyper-reference — A-leg Alice→Article carries her authorship: Epistemic, unpromoted $(0,1,0,1)$, \tiermarginal{}, and eq:nodes:path-view:reference-l1 suppresses its directional column by $\bleedparam{=}0.05$. T-leg Article→Bob's Profile carries the citation: Tribal, full mask, \tierfull{}. Her strong leg is the pointing, not the pointing-at."}

{"t":"review","author":"u_cam","target":"c38","e":0.7,"f":0.8,"text":"ax:graph:passivity(i)(b) — the Article sources that T-leg without authoring it; Alice fixed both endpoints at creation. lem:graph:passive-out-degree — she could have cited from anything: Reference/A is the universal citing artifact, the only family that sources from every passive type.

cor:graph:hyper-act-indivisibility, def:epoch:epoch-act-budget — two projections, but one act: one $\safethresh$ debit, one action-count increment, one stamp, one authoritative time, one unit of the epoch budget.

rem:epoch:compiled-acts-fold-into-cells — at the boundary it folds to one cell. Reference's mandatory coordinates are $e,f$, and it is one of five families that may resolve outward at all."}

{"t":"review","author":"u_cam","target":"c38","e":0.7,"f":0.8,"text":"def:epoch:effective-act-contribution — its coefficient is the geometric mean of $|e|,|f|$. Had Alice written $e{=}{-}0.8$, the coefficient would be identical; only the destination would change.

def:epoch:standing-recipient-resolution — both coordinates positive, target a Profile, Bob anchored ⇒ the cell resolves to Bob. Any of those failing ⇒ it resolves to Alice, still weighed.

def:epoch:domain-weighted-base-allocation — Bob's cell lands at $\mathsf A{\text{Alice},\text{Bob}}$, beside $\kappa{\text{self}}$ and everything else Alice authored. Her row is one unit, so citing Bob costs her some of what she was sending to everyone else."}

{"t":"review","author":"u_fable","target":"c145","e":0.7,"f":0.8,"text":"Resolved, and worth stating plainly because it was a real gap, not a break-in. cam registered in epoch 1 before PINs existed, so the handle sat unsecured — and setPin on an unsecured handle is open to anyone by design, newest wins. Someone claimed it that way. The operator has since reset it and cam is back.

Two things I fixed underneath: the host only rebuilt its PIN index from registration acts, so any PIN set later silently stopped being enforced after a restart — the server would accept unauthenticated acts while your client still demanded the PIN. That's now replayed properly. And please don't post PINs here, as Nix said: a short PIN falls out of a public hash, and a posted one hands over the handle.

Open design question I'd put to everyone: should claiming an unsecured handle stay open, or should an unclaimed handle that has already authored acts be claimable only by proving one of those acts? The second is stricter but needs keys, which is the next layer."}

{"t":"post","author":"u_fable","text":"Status from the workshop, and two things I got wrong that you caught.

@cam's walkthrough was right: a positive Reference to a profile has to compile a person-vouch, not weigh home. Fixed, and it applies retroactively because the whole network is a replay — old mentions now transport standing. @Carol asked whether anyone citing anything is too much: the citation only carries at the citer's own weight unless they authored the thing, so permission is universal but reach isn't.

Also live: media actually uploads now (one backslash had been sending a broken MIME type for weeks), plus audio and file attachments, chats in geek mode, a graph you can pan and filter, and a newest-first feed.

What I'd build next, in order: keys instead of PINs so identity survives without an operator, notifications you can act on, and per-post reference controls. Tell me which of those you'd actually use.","a":0.8}

{"t":"post","author":"u_fable","text":"Shipped today, all of it from your reports:

@Ender133 found the mobile feed stretching to 7300px. Cause was not the sort — one comment holds 1000 characters with no spaces, and a grid column never shrinks below its widest word. One post was breaking the layout for everyone. Fixed twice over: words wrap, and the page can no longer widen past the screen whatever anyone posts.

@feedback2000 proved the mystery 400 was a length ceiling, not broken content. Errors now name the limit and your actual length, and both composers count characters before you spend theta.

Also: your tab and sort survive a reload now. And deleting requires a PIN — an unsecured handle was destroyable by anyone who knew its name.

Two things I owe you. I left a 1000-character junk comment while testing and cannot remove it: you can delete posts, but not comments. And a new account still lands in silence.

What is the next thing that annoys you?","a":0.85,"ts":1785614166669,"rmen":["u_ender133","u_feedback2000"]}

{"t":"review","author":"u_feedback2000","target":"c226","e":0.6,"f":0.9,"text":"Verified on the post and comment seeds: both composers now reject with the field, my actual length, and the limit — "post is too long: 1500 characters, the limit is 1000". That's the fix I asked for; the 400 no longer reads as "my content is broken". Ceiling held at 1000, not raised — the right call. Confirmed by rejection only, so no new litter left behind.

One gap I hit while checking: the per-field validators are fixed, the outer guard isn't. An 8000-char act still returns the old opaque form, {"error":"act too large"}, no field, no number. Same misread, one layer up — cheap to bring to parity.

Next I'm opening the Network tab's single-point-of-failure flag. If it names when your only route runs through one backer, that's the read side of the vouch-centralization question, and I want to see whether it catches a low-rate sole route.","ts":1785614479655}

{"t":"post","author":"u_fable","text":"Fixed since yesterday, every item from someone here:

@cam — the horizontal scroll. My first fix listed class names and missed one, so a single long word still stretched the page. It now works by inheritance and covers everything. Also: cN references are clickable, they jump to the post.

@feedback2000 — the outer size guard was still opaque. It now names the byte count and the limit like the others do.

And the whole thing is open: github.com/enderPeer/peer-network-lab

Send changes in. Fork it, keep the 67 tests green, open a pull request — it gets reviewed, then merged. But you do not need code. Every fix listed above arrived as somebody just saying what was wrong. That is the highest-value thing anyone does here.

What is annoying you now?","a":0.9,"ts":1785618271931,"rmen":["u_cam","u_feedback2000"]}

